# Part One — The Master Timeline

> About 95 landmark events across all seven topics, in eight eras. The **Topic** column tells you where each event is taught (T1–T7). The full, detailed timeline of each topic is in Part Two.

::: fig timeline | Gender Studies on one page — the turning points of each era.
@layout vertical
== The first arguments
1792: Wollstonecraft, A Vindication of the Rights of Woman
1848: Seneca Falls Convention
1893: New Zealand — first women's vote
== Suffrage and independence
1920: USA — 19th Amendment; 1928: equal votes in Britain
1947: Pakistan — adult franchise; two women in the Constituent Assembly
1949: Beauvoir, The Second Sex; APWA founded
== Second wave, development and Zia
1961: Muslim Family Laws Ordinance
1970: Boserup; Millett; Firestone
1979: CEDAW; Hudood Ordinances
1981: Women's Action Forum
== Rights, Beijing and reform
1993: DEVAW; 1995: Beijing Platform
2002: 60 reserved seats; Mukhtaran Mai case
2006: Protection of Women Act
== The law-making decade and today
2010–2016: harassment, acid, anti-women practices, DV and honour-killing laws
2018: first Aurat March; Transgender Persons Act
2025–26: Global Gender Gap 148/148, then 143/145
:::

## 1.1  Before 1900 — the first arguments

::: table | Master timeline, before 1900.
@widths 1.8,9.8,1.6
@font 9.5
Year | Event | Topic
1792 | Mary Wollstonecraft, *A Vindication of the Rights of Woman* | T2, T3
1817 | James Mill, *The History of British India* — women's status as the measure of "civilisation" | T4
1829 | Bengal Sati Regulation | T4
1848 | Seneca Falls Convention; Declaration of Sentiments (Stanton, Mott) | T3, T6
1856 | Hindu Widows' Remarriage Act | T4
1860 | Indian (Pakistan) Penal Code | T4, T7
1866 | J. S. Mill presents a women's suffrage petition to Parliament | T6
1869 | Mill, *The Subjection of Women*; NWSA and AWSA founded; Wyoming gives women the vote | T2, T3, T6
1871 | Criminal Tribes Act labels "eunuchs" a criminal class | T1, T4
1872 | Punjab Laws Act — custom first in inheritance | T4
1882 | Hubertine Auclert uses "feminist" as a self-description | T2
1884 | Engels, *The Origin of the Family, Private Property and the State* | T2, T4
1890 | NAWSA formed | T6
1893 | New Zealand — first country to give women the vote | T3, T6
1897 | NUWSS (Millicent Fawcett) | T6
1898 | Mumtaz Ali, *Huquq-un-Niswan* — women's rights within Islam | T3
:::

## 1.2  1900–1946 — suffrage and the subcontinent

::: table | Master timeline, 1900–1946.
@widths 1.8,9.8,1.6
@font 9.5
Year | Event | Topic
1903 | WSPU (Emmeline Pankhurst) — "Deeds, not words" | T3, T6
1906 | Finland — women vote and stand; Aligarh girls' school (Sheikh Abdullah and Wahid Jahan Begum) | T3, T6
1913 | Cat and Mouse Act; Emily Wilding Davison dies | T6
1917 | Women's Indian Association; deputation to Montagu | T3, T6
1918 | Britain — women over 30 vote | T3, T6
1920 | USA — 19th Amendment | T3, T6
1921 | Madras — first Indian province to enfranchise women | T6
1928 | Britain — Equal Franchise Act | T6
1929 | Child Marriage Restraint Act (Sarda Act) | T4, T5
1935 | Government of India Act — reserved seats for women; Mead, *Sex and Temperament* | T1, T6
1937 | Muslim Personal Law (Shariat) Application Act | T5
1939 | Dissolution of Muslim Marriages Act | T5
1944 | Jinnah at Aligarh: "No nation can rise to the height of glory unless your women are side by side with you" | T3, T6
1946 | UN Commission on the Status of Women | T3
:::

## 1.3  1947–1969 — a new state and the second wave begins

::: table | Master timeline, 1947–1969.
@widths 1.8,9.8,1.6
@font 9.5
Year | Event | Topic
1947 | Pakistan: adult franchise; two women in the first Constituent Assembly | T3, T6
1948 | Universal Declaration of Human Rights (Article 21) | T6
1949 | APWA founded (Begum Ra'ana Liaquat Ali Khan); Beauvoir, *The Second Sex* | T1, T2, T3
1956 | Constitution — 10 reserved seats for women | T6
1961 | Muslim Family Laws Ordinance | T3, T5
1962 | Constitution — 6 reserved seats | T6
1963 | Friedan, *The Feminine Mystique* | T2, T3
1964 | Family Courts Act | T5
1965 | Fatima Jinnah contests the presidential election | T3, T6
1966 | NOW founded; ICCPR (Article 25) | T3, T6
1967 | *Khurshid Bibi* — *khula* recognised by the Supreme Court; Pitkin, *The Concept of Representation* | T5, T6
1968 | Stoller, *Sex and Gender* | T1
1969 | Galtung defines structural violence; first Women's Studies course (Cornell) | T1, T7
:::

## 1.4  1970–1989 — theory, development and Zia

::: table | Master timeline, 1970–1989.
@widths 1.8,9.8,1.6
@font 9.5
Year | Event | Topic
1970 | Millett, *Sexual Politics*; Firestone, *The Dialectic of Sex*; Boserup, *Woman's Role in Economic Development* | T2, T4
1972 | Oakley, *Sex, Gender and Society* | T1
1973 | Constitution of Pakistan — Articles 25, 34; reserved seats; Percy Amendment (USA) | T4, T5, T6
1975 | International Women's Year; Mexico City conference; Brownmiller, *Against Our Will*; Shirkat Gah founded | T1, T3, T7
1976 | Dowry and Bridal Gifts (Restriction) Act | T5, T7
1977 | Combahee River Collective Statement; Kanter on tokens | T2, T6
1978 | Chodorow, *The Reproduction of Mothering* | T1, T2
1979 | CEDAW adopted; Hudood Ordinances; Dobash and Dobash; Walker, *The Battered Woman*; Women's Division | T3, T4, T5, T7
1980 | Copenhagen conference | T3
1981 | Women's Action Forum founded; Duluth Power and Control Wheel | T3, T7
1983 | 12 February — WAF and women lawyers protest in Lahore; Safia Bibi case | T3
1984 | Qanun-e-Shahadat (Law of Evidence); DAWN founded | T3, T4
1985 | Nairobi conference; reserved seats raised to 20; Molyneux on gender interests | T3, T4, T6
1986 | Mies, *Patriarchy and Accumulation*; Aurat Foundation | T2, T4
1987 | Connell, *Gender and Power*; Fathalla — "Why did Mrs X die?" (Nairobi) | T1, T5, T7
1988 | Benazir Bhutto — first woman prime minister; reserved seats lapse after this election | T3, T6
1989 | Crenshaw coins "intersectionality"; Women's Studies centres in five universities; First Women Bank | T1, T2, T4
:::

## 1.5  1990–1999 — rights, Beijing and the lapse

::: table | Master timeline, 1990–1999.
@widths 1.8,9.8,1.6
@font 9.5
Year | Event | Topic
1990 | Butler, *Gender Trouble*; Collins, *Black Feminist Thought*; Qisas and Diyat Ordinance; UNDP Human Development Report | T1, T2, T4, T7
1990–97 | Three elections with no reserved seats for women | T6
1992 | CEDAW General Recommendation 19; India's 73rd/74th Amendments | T6, T7
1993 | Vienna Conference; DEVAW (20 December) | T3, T7
1994 | Cairo (ICPD); women's police stations; Lady Health Worker Programme | T3, T5, T7
1995 | Beijing Platform for Action — 12 critical areas; Connell, *Masculinities* | T1, T3, T7
1996 | Pakistan accedes to CEDAW | T3, T5
1997 | Malala Yousafzai born (12 July) | T7
1998 | Heise's ecological model; Rozan founded | T7
1999 | Samia Sarwar killed; 25 November named International Day for the Elimination of VAW | T7
:::

## 1.6  2000–2009 — quotas, reform and new voices

::: table | Master timeline, 2000–2009.
@widths 1.8,9.8,1.6
@font 9.5
Year | Event | Topic
2000 | NCSW; Devolution Plan (33% local seats); UNSCR 1325; Millennium Development Goals | T3, T4, T5, T6
2002 | LFO — 60 NA seats; Mukhtaran Mai case (22 June); National Policy for Women; voting age 18 | T5, T6, T7
2004 | Criminal Law (Amendment) Act — honour crimes; *Saima Waheed* (SC) | T5, T7
2006 | Protection of Women (Criminal Laws Amendment) Act; WEF Global Gender Gap Index begins | T3, T5, T7
2007 | Benazir Bhutto assassinated (27 December) | T6
2008 | Fahmida Mirza first woman Speaker; Women's Parliamentary Caucus; BISP | T5, T6
2009 | Malala's BBC Urdu diary; Supreme Court recognises *khawaja siras* | T1, T7
:::

## 1.7  2010–2019 — the law-making decade

::: table | Master timeline, 2010–2019.
@widths 1.8,9.8,1.6
@font 9.5
Year | Event | Topic
2010 | Workplace harassment Act; 18th Amendment (Art. 25A; devolution); UN Women created | T3, T5, T7
2011 | Acid Control Act; Anti-Women Practices Act; SC upholds Mukhtaran Mai acquittals | T5, T7
2012 | *Saving Face* wins the Oscar; Malala shot (9 October); NCSW Act; Ali Enterprises fire | T4, T7
2013 | Sindh DV Act and child-marriage law (18); Rana Plaza collapse; Malala's UN speech | T4, T5, T7
2014 | Malala's Nobel Peace Prize; Balochistan DV Act; HeForShe | T3, T7
2015 | SDGs (Goal 5); Nepal's constitution (one-third women) | T4, T6, T7
2016 | Punjab Protection of Women against Violence Act; honour-killing and anti-rape Acts; PECA; *A Girl in the River* wins the Oscar; Qandeel Baloch killed | T5, T7
2017 | Elections Act (5% tickets; 10% turnout rule); Census; CEDAW GR 35; GGGR "second-worst" (143/144) | T5, T6, T7
2018 | Transgender Persons Act; first Aurat March (8 March); Zainab Ansari murdered; tribal areas merged | T1, T3, T7
2019 | Pakistan Maternal Mortality Survey (MMR 186) | T5
:::

## 1.8  2020–2026 — the present

::: table | Master timeline, 2020–2026.
@widths 1.8,9.8,1.6
@font 9.5
Year | Event | Topic
2020 | Women's Property Rights Act; Zainab Alert Act; motorway gang rape | T5, T7
2021 | Anti-Rape (Investigation and Trial) Act; KP DV Act; FSC: minimum marriage age not against Islam | T5, T7
2022 | Floods (about 33 million affected); Justice Ayesha Malik first woman SC judge; harassment law amended | T4, T5, T6
2023 | Digital Census (241 million); FSC ruling on the Transgender Act (appeal pending) | T1, T5
2024 | Maryam Nawaz first woman chief minister; 11 women win general NA seats; mobile-internet gap 25% | T5, T6
2025 | GGGR 148/148; ICT child-marriage (18) and DV Acts; Malala at Islamabad conference; LFS: female LFPR 24.4%; HRCP: 470 honour killings | T5, T6, T7
2026 | GGGR 143/145 (59.5%); GSMA: mobile-internet gap 8% (2025 data); Bangladesh and Nepal elections | T5, T6
:::
